('','webform.webform.quiz_mediator','a:21:{s:4:\"uuid\";s:36:\"8a270556-92ee-468e-9aef-adfb03705fab\";s:8:\"langcode\";s:2:\"en\";s:6:\"status\";s:4:\"open\";s:12:\"dependencies\";a:1:{s:6:\"module\";a:1:{i:0;s:10:\"vaxia_core\";}}s:6:\"weight\";i:0;s:4:\"open\";N;s:5:\"close\";N;s:3:\"uid\";i:0;s:8:\"template\";b:0;s:7:\"archive\";b:0;s:2:\"id\";s:13:\"quiz_mediator\";s:5:\"title\";s:17:\"Mediator Training\";s:11:\"description\";N;s:10:\"categories\";a:0:{}s:8:\"elements\";s:20526:\"question_1:\n  \'#title\': \'You are in limbo, waiting patiently to play in ...\'\n  \'#description\': \"You are in limbo, waiting patiently to play in a session that has signups at capacity. A player who isn\'t on as often comes into limbo and demands that someone step out so that they can play. They say that since they aren\'t on as often they should be given priority so that they can make this session. Other players in Limbo are clearly becoming upset and defensive about the original player\'s remarks. What would your first action be? Would you follow up with the player in any way?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_2:\n  \'#title\': \'During a session, Player A  has a character that attacks ...\'\n  \'#description\': \"During a session, Player A  has a character that attacks Player B\'s character, seemingly out of nowhere. There\'s no clear IC reason for them to have done so, and the session is paused while the A/SH running it sorts out what\'s happened. The A/SH manages to get the session going again, without the attack occurring so that the other players can still enjoy the session. Unfortunately Player A and Player B instead move their fight into Limbo and begin arguing there instead. It\'s quickly becoming both distracting to the other players and the A/SH, and it\'s becoming very toxic. What (if anything) would you do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_3:\n  \'#title\': \'There are two players who have seemed to never ...\'\n  \'#description\': \"There are two players who have seemed to never get along. Finally the other players are tired of seeing them constantly bickering, and the site agrees that it\'s gone far enough and they need a mediation to start being civil. As the primary mediator you are in charge of keeping the conversation focused, while one of the leads is there to oversee in case things get out of hand. The players come in and the mediation starts off poorly. It quickly becomes a chance for the two of them to argue and point fingers about which of the two of them is at fault for their personal issues. During the mediation, they are both in violation of several Code of Conduct rules. After numerous messages it seems as though there may not be any progress toward getting the two players to get along. How would you proceed?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_4:\n  \'#title\': \'You are overseeing a mediation between two players ...\'\n  \'#description\': \"You are overseeing a mediation between two players. During the mediation you step in to ask one of the players to watch their language. They\'re angry and are having outbursts that are not conducive to solving their problems. The Player then begins to argue with you. The second mediator steps in as well, and a similar outcome occurs with the player arguing with the second mediator. In detail, what would you do next?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_5:\n  \'#title\': \'Here is an example of an exchange between players ...\'\n  \'#description\': \"Here is an example of an exchange between players:<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Player A:</strong> My dice always fail me when I need them.<br>\\n<strong>Player B:</strong> It wasn\'t your dice. It was your plan. That plan you had was not going to work.<br>\\n<strong>Player A:</strong> It would have worked. That\'s not fair. I was thinking outside of the box. Which is still better than your plan of sitting on the sidelines complaining about people trying to make a plan. You always do that. You talk about other people, but you don\'t see how stupid your actions are.<br>\\n<strong>Player B:</strong> My actions aren\'t stupid. I\'m playing my character the way they\'re written. So you can\'t say that I\'m playing them wrong. You never play a consistent character. You\'re always just doing whatever you think is funny. It\'s going to make it so people don\'t want to play with you anymore.<br>\\n<strong>Player A:</strong> You have no idea what you\'re talking about. Don\'t threaten me.<br>\\n<strong>Player B:</strong> I didn\'t threaten you. I\'m just done with talking to you.<br>\\n<br>\\nWhat, if anything, would you do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_6:\n  \'#title\': \'Learning to be a passive participant in mediations is ...\'\n  \'#description\': \"Learning to be a passive participant in mediations is important. Remember that you aren\'t there in the mediation to force a resolution. You\'re there to help guide the players having the conflict to resolve their issues at their own pace. This requires finesse and patience. Work on asking fewer questions, and when you do, ask open-ended questions. Compare \\\"Did you  argue?\\\" to \\\"What happened?\\\" Or, \\\"Do you feel anxious about the session tomorrow?\\\" to \\\"How do you feel about the session tomorrow?\\\" An open-ended question is like an essay question which allows the player in the mediation to lead the conversation and clarify his or her own concerns. A closed question is like a true/false question and often suggests or narrows the focus of the Mediation.<br>\\n<br>\\nPlease give some examples of open ended questions and times when you would use them.\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_7:\n  \'#title\': \'There will be times when mediations are not enough...\'\n  \'#description\': \"There will be times when mediations are not enough. When that occurs you will often be tasked with figuring out an alternate solution to help the players work through their issues. Try to focus on their specific problems. Whether their biggest issues are IC or OOC, you should be able to find a method that keeps them happy, even if they cannot be friendly to each other.<br>\\n<br>\\nHowever it is possible that the players will agree to some sort of compromise, and then be unwilling to see it through. Perhaps they feel that agreeing to some sort of action will allow them to get back to the game quicker, but they never truly plan on committing to the solution.  <br>\\n<br>\\nFor example, they might agree to a rotating schedule, but one player keeps showing up saying that they forgot. Or they might agree to separate casual RP but one of the players keeps arriving anyway and forcing the other player to leave. This causes their previous issues to flare back up again. When the compromises that they agreed to are broken, what would you do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_8:\n  \'#title\': \'Sometimes a mediation will be needed after two players...\'\n  \'#description\': \"Sometimes a mediation will be needed after two players have a particularly hurtful exchange onsite. This could be the sort of thing that happens frequently between the same two players, or it could have been something that was sudden and due to external stimuli of some sort. In either case, there will be times that an apology needs to be tendered by one or more people in the mediation. Most often, people know how to legitimately apologize. Especially after they have calmed down from the initial outburst. But there are times when it can seem disingenuous. Don\'t hesitate to pause the mediation and ask the player who isn\'t quite hitting the mark to consider ways to re-word their apology.<br>\\n<br>\\nAn apology is an act that is neither about problem-solving or negotiation. Rather, it is a way to help gain closure on an issue so that the mediation can move forward into discussion about how to prevent problems in the future. The goal is to allow players to authentically apologize in mediation, but they often need help in getting past the defensiveness and fear of blame that preclude apology. To show genuine remorse is hard in text, so there will be issues occasionally. There are many more steps that can occur afterward, but an apology should be one of the first steps when applicable to the situation. Neither side is required to accept the apology given to them. But if the apology is genuine, they should at least attempt to consider it an olive branch in terms of their future interactions onsite. Apology can not be imposed or forced. It is a moment of opportunity. But know that players may need preparation and help with the words.<br>\\n<br>\\nThis is a two part question. There will be two hypothetical situations. Please describe how you would respond to both of them.<br>\\n<br>\\nSituation A: The two players involved in the mediation have a long standing history of being snide and snippy with each other. Recently it developed into harassment, both IC and OOC. Some of the words used and actions were incredibly hurtful. One player\'s apologies seem less sincere, and are often followed up with a caveat or a qualifier. How would you help guide  them to make their apologies seem legitimate?<br>\\n<br>\\nSituation B: A player is having a bad day offsite and they come on and ask for advice. During the exchange, one player says something inappropriate, and due to the offsite stress, the player who was having a bad day snaps and uses hurtful language. They are pulled aside and asked to calm down. Once they do so, they feel legitimate remorse, but come to you as a mediator to ask for help in wording their apology. What help would you offer to them?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_9:\n  \'#title\': \'This is a hypothetical situation that occurs onsite...\'\n  \'#description\': \"This is a hypothetical situation that occurs onsite.<br>\\n<br>\\nOn the forums, two players are having a discussion about character development and goals. The first player asks for clarification on an issue, and the second player responds.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Player A:</strong> I\'m still confused about why characters can\'t travel between the Science Fiction and Fantasy realms. I mean, it\'s the same place, right?<br>\\n<strong>Player B: </strong>It\'s not. That\'s like asking about a fan theory. Like why Superman doesn\'t just get (or let) someone else to kill Lex Luthor. Or why the token in Inception keeps spinning even though the top was his wife\'s token.<br>\\n<strong>Player A: </strong>That\'s not very productive, and it was a complete derail of my question.<br>\\n<strong>Player B:</strong> Well, I was just trying to say that you were wrong about that theory. Posting those sorts of ideas here on the forums is tantamount to fanfiction in my eyes.<br>\\n<strong>Player A:</strong> I don\'t even understand that.<br>\\n<br>\\nThe conversation then moves back toward the original discussion. What, if anything, would you do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_10:\n  \'#title\': \'Our Code of Conduct states that we have a zero tolerance...\'\n  \'#description\': \"Our Code of Conduct states that we have a zero tolerance for certain behaviors. This document is important to know as a mediator, so please make sure that you\'ve read and understand it. Often times it\'s a very valuable tool you have to help people during a mediation. So make sure you keep the site policies close at hand during your mediations. <br>\\n<br>\\n/wiki/site-policies<br>\\n<br>\\nNow, if we lived in a perfect world, there would be no need for mediation. Everyone would be happy and they would get along all the time. But sadly, even a fun game like ours isn\'t immune to real world stress. We are all going to have bad days. Sometimes players will avoid the site when they are hurting just so that they don\'t take their frustrations out on others. But there will be times when they come to the site angry and they hurt another player, either IC or OOC or both. Sometimes a player will come to you and ask for help after the event occurred because they are continuing to feel upset.<br>\\n<br>\\nHere is another situation.<br>\\n<br>\\nPlayer A messages you privately a few days after they received a private message from Player B. Player A wishes to get help or advice because the message that they recieved felt hurtful to them. They attach a copy of the message at the end of their note to you and it is filled with hurtful defamatory language. Far surpassing anything that our site policy allows. Player A is scared of ramifications if they come forward with this message, but they\'re also afraid of what Player B might say to them in the future.<br>\\n<br>\\n Explain what you would do if you were faced with this conflict.\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_11:\n  \'#title\': \'As a mediator you need to be on the lookout for possible...\'\n  \'#description\': \"As a mediator you need to be on the lookout for possible traps that players will set for you. It is unlikely to occur, but there will be times that a player will try to trap you or the other player.  They may attempt to get out of their responsibility by forcing you into a position where you say something without realizing the double meaning. Here are some types of traps to look out for  during mediations.<br>\\n<br>\\n-The Prediction Trap: Asking you to predict or guess what might occur, and then considering it to be fact rather than speculation. Do not be overly cautious or overly confident when you enter into a mediation. If you do this, you run the risk of extending the mediation. The best course of action during a \\\"Prediction Trap\\\" is to explain that you cannot speculate, and ask them a question to get the focus back onto the mediation and their role in it.<br>\\n<br>\\n-The Framing Trap: Causing you to use your own past experiences to adjust (or frame) your own view of the conflict in order to better suit their needs. Remember, in a mediation you must always be neutral and try not to consider anything but the current situation.<br>\\n<br>\\n-The Comfort Zone Trap: It is possible that if you have already helped with a mediation in which the outcome was [X] you may find yourself leaning toward that same resolution in the future. Remember that every conflict is different. If you always advocate for one specific resolution players may attempt to use that to their advantage.<br>\\n<br>\\n-The Supporting Evidence Trap: Having a clear view of both sides of an argument is vital. If one player seems particularly willing to give ancillary evidence that they\'re right, you\'ll want to make sure you get copies from both players if you can. It can be exceedingly hard to tell who\'s telling the truth so make sure that you don\'t just get the story from one side of the mediation. You can always ask the technical admin to confirm the onsite communications. If something seems amiss, it is very wise to double check.<br>\\n<br>\\nThere are other types of course. But they are rare enough that you hopefully won\'t face any of these traps. Paying close attention to the wording and possible outcomes from your answers will help you spot a situation like this before it becomes an issue.<br>\\n<br>\\nHow would you respond to the following situation. You are mediating for two players. One of whom is also a mediator. Player A has worked on mediations before and knows that showing evidence can be key to getting out of a problem, so they do so in abundance. Even including things that may not be relevant to further cloud the issue. They also occasionally pepper in phrases aimed toward you like \\\"You know me\\\" or \\\"I\'m trained to help people.\\\" Whether intentionally or not, they seem to be putting you into a situation where you or the other mediator may be trapped. How would you respond to the situation?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_12:\n  \'#title\': \'Sometimes the site will have a player who shows repeated...\'\n  \'#description\': \"Sometimes the site will have a player who shows repeated instances of difficult behavior. Difficult behavior is essentially that which inhibits the productivity, site enjoyment, and well being of others. Left alone it will get worse, affect more people and continue to cause ripples of conflict throughout the site. Most difficult behavior is accidental, but it can also be the result of intentional thought. Sometimes it is sporadic and takes us by surprise. At other times it is ongoing and forms patterns. It\'s important to remember that difficult behavior can take many forms as well. It includes gossiping with the intent to get someone else in trouble, going over an SH\'s head, foot dragging after a request is made, outright ignoring a request that was made, being rude, \\\"yelling\\\" at other players, ignoring others to cause them to feel shunned, harassment of any sort, and much more. You\'ll know difficult behavior when you see it even if it\'s not on this list.<br>\\n<br>\\nHere are some suggestions on ways to help deal with difficult behavior.<br>\\n<br>\\n-Stay centered. Don\'t let your personal feelings affect your response. As a mediator, you must remain neutral at all times no matter how hard you\'re pushed. If you can\'t be neutral, then pass the mediation to someone else. It\'s better than taking the low road that you have been goaded into.<br>\\n<br>\\n-Give yourself a reality check. Is it possible that the player\'s behavior isn\'t as harmful, but you\'re compounding it based on past events? If so, try to take a step back and once again only look at the current situation. If a pattern has emerged, that\'s a different story, but if you\'re finding an isolated event to be bigger than it normally would be, you may be feeling compromised yourself.<br>\\n<br>\\n-Focus on the behavior, and not the player. If you focus on the behavior as a separate entity it\'s more likely that the player will be open to change. If they feel personally targeted, they will be less likely to adjust their behavior. Remember, be soft on the player, and hard on the behavior.<br>\\n<br>\\n-Listen carefully. It\'s easy to get swept up in the chaos that can come along with disruptive and difficult behavior. The key in all mediation is to listen. Often times the root of the problem is hiding right behind the wall of static that is being caused. If you listen carefully you will be able to help more effectively.<br>\\n<br>\\n-Give feedback directly and honestly to the player. A common problem with difficult behavior is that the person is unaware that his or her behavior is causing a problem. At other times the extent of the impact is not comprehended. By giving timely feedback about specific behavior misunderstanding can be avoided and expectations clarified<br>\\n<br>\\n-Consider bringing in an additional mediator. Sometimes it helps to get more than one person to brainstorm ways to effectively alter difficult behavior without causing the player harm. During a full mediation you will always have at least one lead already in the discussion. But if you find yourself without another Mediator or lead, feel free to get one to help. They won\'t be there just to \'back you up\\\" but they will be there to offer a fresh set of eyes.<br>\\n<br>\\n-Using formal authority should only be as a last resort. Don\'t immediately pull out the card that you\'re in a position of authority over them. This will cause the player to view everything that occurs afterward under the lens of a hierarchy. While they might initially listen, they will eventually revert to the previous behavior and be even less likely to change due to the power imbalance they now feel exists.<br>\\n<br>\\n-Remember we\'re all humans here. The reality is that we can all be difficult from time to time. So treat others with the respect you would want if you were in their shoes. Dealing with difficult behavior is not easy and so we often procrastinate. We do so at our own peril. Being proactive and engaging the person in a conversation about their behavior is the first step toward conflict resolution.  <br>\\n<br>\\nNow then, considering the above, here is the question. You have a player that is constantly harassing other players on site. There seems to be no logical pattern to the timing, but they are constantly stepping on toes. This causes people to react back to them, often in poorly chosen ways. So other players are now also getting in trouble for the original player\'s outbursts. This makes the original player even more vocal about how they\'re right. They are getting attention and after some reinforcement of their bad behavior they show no signs of stopping. What would you do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': textarea\n  \'#required\': true\nactions:\n  \'#submit__label\': Finish\n  \'#type\': webform_actions\n  \'#title\': \'Submit button(s)\'\n\";s:3:\"css\";s:0:\"\";s:10:\"javascript\";s:0:\"\";s:8:\"settings\";a:134:{s:4:\"ajax\";b:0;s:15:\"ajax_scroll_top\";s:4:\"form\";s:18:\"ajax_progress_type\";s:0:\"\";s:11:\"ajax_effect\";s:0:\"\";s:10:\"ajax_speed\";N;s:4:\"page\";b:1;s:16:\"page_submit_path\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"page_confirm_path\";s:0:\"\";s:15:\"page_theme_name\";s:0:\"\";s:10:\"form_title\";s:4:\"both\";s:16:\"form_submit_once\";b:0;s:17:\"form_open_message\";s:0:\"\";s:18:\"form_close_message\";s:0:\"\";s:22:\"form_exception_message\";s:0:\"\";s:25:\"form_previous_submissions