in-setting-department]Join the department here.[/url]\";s:15:\"summary_default\";s:74:\"Not quite. Have another look at the realm pages on the wiki and try again.\";s:5:\"takes\";i:0;s:19:\"summary_pass_format\";s:4:\"wiki\";s:22:\"summary_default_format\";s:4:\"wiki\";}}}s:8:\"variants\";a:0:{}}'),
('','webform.webform.quiz_walkthrough_social','a:21:{s:4:\"uuid\";s:36:\"8d7350e4-7fc9-4552-aa19-22a7e6402802\";s:8:\"langcode\";s:2:\"en\";s:6:\"status\";s:4:\"open\";s:12:\"dependencies\";a:1:{s:6:\"module\";a:1:{i:0;s:10:\"vaxia_core\";}}s:6:\"weight\";i:0;s:4:\"open\";N;s:5:\"close\";N;s:3:\"uid\";i:0;s:8:\"template\";b:0;s:7:\"archive\";b:0;s:2:\"id\";s:23:\"quiz_walkthrough_social\";s:5:\"title\";s:30:\"New Player: Social Walkthrough\";s:11:\"description\";N;s:10:\"categories\";a:0:{}s:8:\"elements\";s:36812:\"question_1:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Diversity of Play\'\n  \'#description\': \"Not all players are playing the same game you are. We all come from different experiences in life. Some of our players have never played outside of this site, some started play in the olden days when Dungeons &amp; Dragons was the only game in town. Some come from the point of view of wanting to play a dramatic story and the day-to-day lives of their characters, while others want to focus on action, adventure, and building a legend for themselves.<br>\\n<br>\\nIt’s important to note: All of these are completely legitimate ways to play!<br>\\n<br>\\nWith the many varieties of players as we have, we’re bound to find players whose personal styles just don’t naturally click. There will be misunderstandings along the way. But there’s some steps you can take to help smooth the rough edges. One key element is to remember to make sure your fellow players want to go along with your idea.<br>\\n<br>\\nIt’s a simple thing: When in doubt, ask.<br>\\n<br>\\nAnd if you are asked, give an honest and clear answer. Not speaking up once you’ve been asked isn’t helpful to anyone.  If you aren’t sure what type of play you prefer, let folks know, and be prepared for the chance that you might discover a style you like (or don’t like) in play.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\n<br>\\nIf you\'re roleplaying a scene in a bar, and you notice other characters are getting agitated with your behavior and a player even has their character leave the scene early, what should you do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \'Check in Limbo if the other players are ok with the play, if not, rein the character in or have them exit the scene so other people can keep playing.\'\n    a2: \'Rein the character in since it seems to be driving players off.\'\n    a3: \"You\'re just playing the character how the character would behave, so nothing to change.\"\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_2:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Gamer types\'\n  \'#description\': \"Robin D. Laws is a game designer and writer who wrote a book called “Robin\'s Laws of Good Gamemastering”. We can’t recommend this book nearly enough if you’re interested in sharpening your skills as a player and a game designer or game master. It’s mostly common sense advice. But one of the most valuable tools in Laws’s book is the “gamer types”.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>The Power Gamer:</strong> Get more powers and use them often and efficiently.<br>\\n<strong>The Butt-Kicker:</strong> Enjoys combat and pwning NPCs!<br>\\n<strong>The Tactician:</strong> Likes to beat complex situations through thought and planning.<br>\\n<strong>The Specialist:</strong> The one who always plays a \\\"insert character type\\\". Ninjas and Dritzz clones are popular.<br>\\n<strong>The Method Actor:</strong> Likes total immersion in a character’s assumed persona, whatever the costs!<br>\\n<strong>The Storyteller:</strong> Enjoys exploring a story unfold around a character’s actions and choices.<br>\\n<strong>The Casual Gamer:</strong> Shows up to be with friends and share the social energies of the group.<br>\\n<br>\\nMore information on Robin’s gamer types and the traits people come to a table for <a href=\\\"http://www.critical-hits.com/blog/2008/01/23/robins-laws-revisited-part-2-player-types-and-traits/\\\">can be found online</a>.<br>\\n<br>\\nMost players are a combination of types! So it can be handy to know roughly where you fit. It helps you identify what sort of characters and sessions you’re looking for. If you’re playing with another player and not getting along, make sure you’re on the same page - the other player may be looking for more of a storyteller scene while you’re looking for a way to blow some steam off after a rough day. Try to find common ground.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nWhat kind of gamer are you (mostly)?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \'Power Gamer\'\n    a2: Butt-kicker\n    a3: Tactician\n    a4: Specialist\n    a5: \'Method Actor\'\n    a6: Storyteller\n    a7: \'Casual Gamer\'\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_3:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - OOC vs IC\'\n  \'#description\': \"You’ll see “OOC” and “IC” floating around a lot on the site. Those stand for “Out Of Character” and “In Character”.<br>\\n<br>\\nOut of character is stuff you, as the player know about and consider. In character is stuff your character knows. You know both the OOC and IC, but your character only knows the IC. That means, your character shouldn’t use any of your OOC knowledge. If you need to take notes - do that! Plenty of people do. Each character sheet has a section for “Player Notes” - that’s for you to write down anything you need to.<br>\\n<br>\\nPlaying two of your characters at once - we really really don’t recommend you do this. Not only is it a bit presumptuous to assume your character’s know each other, but it puts you in a sticky spot. You’re your own best friend - that means you’re in conflict of interest for any favors your characters do for each other. It’s a form of cheating! Your characters shouldn’t share information, silver, or items. Even if it’s innocent, just chattering with yourself, we really don’t recommend making a habit of it - it looks bad, and it won’t be long before someone asks if you are using OOC knowledge.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nWhen can I share loot between two of my own characters? (check all that apply)\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': checkboxes\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: Never.\n    a2: \'If they knew each other before they got the loot.\'\n    a3: \"If they knew each other before, and one roleplayed calling \'dibs\' on the loot for the other who wasn\'t in the session.\"\n    a4: \'If another character made the suggestion.\'\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_4:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Types of RP\'\n  \'#description\': \"There are two types of RP on the site, casual and session.<br>\\n<br>\\nSession RP is when one of our A/SHs plans and runs a scenario for one or more players on the site. Sessions are our version of ‘an adventure’, or even a ‘campaign’ in the case of sagas. In sessions you get the extra challenge and risk of the situation the A/SH has put together for you - but you can also reap plenty of rewards: Silver, extra XP, and items.<br>\\n<br>\\nCasual RP is when you and another player (or half a dozen) decide to take over a room and just have fun! Casual RP can be a lot of fun and it builds the social connections between all of your characters. It also helps you unlock any banked XP you’ve gotten from sessions - so if someone’s in Limbo asking for RP and you’ve an evening free: Take them up on it.<br>\\n<br>\\nSince session RP can only be unlocked with roleplaying posts - to get all the XP your character\'s are entitled to you want to do both casual and session RP.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nWhich type of RP requires having an A/SH on hand?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: Session\n    a2: Casual\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_5:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - How to ask for RP\'\n  \'#description\': \"Sometimes roleplay just happens, however there are times we may have to let people know that we are interested in doing some roleplay. The easiest and most obvious way is to politely ask if anyone would like to come and roleplay with you. The most common place to ask this would be in Limbo as many of us hang out there during the course of our day in order to be with our friends here.<br>\\n<br>\\nWhen asking to roleplay try to include things like which character you are thinking about playing and a possible location. Be ready to offer more than one location or character if the other player doesn’t have one that matched your first suggestion. Work together.<br>\\n<br>\\nRoleplay can also be arranged in the forums or through PMs as well. In the forums there is an In Character section where people can post things pertaining to events that may happen in the IC world. Usually this is a place to post letters from one character to another, or advertise flyers or poster a character may be placing. People can reply with their character’s reactions which may lead to a schedule meeting for roleplay. Sessions will often be posted in the General Discussion area of the forums so people can sign up.<br>\\n<br>\\nOn occasion schedules just don’t sync up, after all we have people from all over the world playing here! This is where slow posting comes in. Slow posting roleplay can be arranged over the forums or PMs as well as in limbo. It works just like normal roleplaying does – just at a much slower pace. For <a href=\\\"/wiki/slow-posting\\\">more information about slow posting go here</a>.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nIf you work third shift and can never quite make regular sessions, what should you do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \'Contact specific players over PMs or forums, to ask if they would like to start slow-posts for RP.\'\n    a2: \'Ask for RP in Limbo, and then stick around for a few hours in case someone comes in who can play.\'\n    a3: \"Ask for RP in Limbo, but if someone isn\'t responding in fifteen minutes go elsewhere.\"\n    a4: \'Nothing - clearly no one wants to take the time to play with me.\'\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_6:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Starting and Ending RP\'\n  \'#description\': \"Joining an existing RP isn\'t as simple as just jumping into a post. It\'s very possible the RP you\'re trying to get in on is a personal scene the players have arranged in advance, and one that\'s very important to one or more of their characters. You can\'t really tell from the outside.<br>\\n<br>\\nIt is always polite to ask before joining first!<br>\\n<br>\\nJust drop a OOC post into the room along the lines of \\\"Anyone mind if I join?\\\" If someone does, don\'t take it personal - some RP is like that. You\'ll catch them on the next opportunity.<br>\\n<br>\\nIf a session is in play, or about to be in place, drop a line to the A/SH in Limbo asking to join. Remember a lot of A/SHs will customize their session based on their headcount up to a day before the session - so by joining late you are rattling their plans - if they say no, don\'t take it personal. If at all possible - make sure to sign up 24 hours in advance for sessions to give them plenty of time to plan.<br>\\n<br>\\nOnce you’re done with a room it’s polite to say thank you to your RP partner, and make it clear you’re done playing. Not just because it’s nice to say thank you - but also because it makes it easier for other players to tell when you’re done with a slow post in a room.<br>\\n<br>\\nA slow-post may have a day or more between each post. If you’re in one be patient. And if you let others know when you’re done that’s a good way to make sure you aren’t keeping others from using the location.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nHow often should I remind another player about a slow-post we\'re both in?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \'After a day or two has passed.\'\n    a2: \'Immediately after I post on the thread.\'\n    a3: \"After an hour without a response on the thread, in case they didn\'t see it.\"\n    a4: \'The next morning.\'\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_7:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Conflicts and PVP\'\n  \'#description\': \"Not all players are comfortable with PvP, or if they are, not on all their characters. That’s ok.<br>\\n<br>\\nWe want to support both PvP and non-PvP play styles on the site, but we respect the real life harm that can come with mixing those groups. If you are getting into a situation where that looks like it may be a possibility - pause first and ask. That’s being respectful of your fellow players who might not otherwise speak up.<br>\\n<br>\\nSimilarly, if you see a situation coming and already know that you yourself are ok with getting into the rough stuff - let your fellow players know. Simply knowing that you’re ok with getting the whuppin’ you’ve earned is a great way to let your characters get on with the scene, without making everyone else nervous that you’re about be upset or hurt out of character.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nIf you\'re about to start a barfight in RP, what\'s the first thing you should do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \"Ask the other player if they\'re ok with this.\"\n    a2: \'Make sure a SH (not just an A/SH) is online to help out.\'\n    a3: \'Make sure you have an IC reason to start the fight.\'\n    a4: \"Nothing - it\'s what my character would do.\"\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_8:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Playing evil\'\n  \'#description\': \"Want to play an evil character?<br>\\n<br>\\nGo right ahead!  But understand this can be tricky. Unlike your usual tabletop game, here players and characters will walk away if things get unpleasant enough. If your character is routinely abusive you should expect the same results you would get in real life for being a jerk. Which can make for some very boring solo roleplay for the rest of the character’s life.<br>\\n<br>\\nPlaying evil requires walking a fine line between offending or harming characters and offending or harming their players.  <br>\\n<br>\\nLastly, remember that evil people have friends too! Disney villains aren’t the most common of evils out there, and rarely does a character (or a person) cast themselves as the ‘bad guy’ of their own story. Evil characters should still be fully developed personalities, with motives and desires - and friends. <br>\\n<br>\\nThat means they will occasionally sacrifice being evil for helping a friend out or being generally agreeable, if only to a handful of people (who may be just as villainous as they are).  That will help keep your character complete, and often make them more likable to players, which should help ensure you can participate in RP with others without constantly chasing them off or getting your character in inescapable trouble (like death or jail).<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nWhat sort of response should I expect for a character who treats everyone around them like servants?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \'The other characters will probably walk off and stop playing with me. They may even let my character get killed in a session.\'\n    a2: \"The other characters have to stay around me, I\'m a player just like they are.\"\n    a3: \"The other characters may walk off if they want, but they have to have my back if we\'re in a session, I\'m a player just like they are.\"\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_9:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Text communication\'\n  \'#description\': \"It is extremely difficult for other people reading your text to  know if you mean your character’s actions to be taken in a positive light or not. A large part of communication simply does not translate to text. For example the facial expression or tone of voice that lets the other person know you’re teasing.<br>\\n<br>\\nSo it can be reassuring for other players to hear you state that you know that an action your character took - is in fact unwise, reckless or evil. It reassures them that you know the difference between IC and OOC, and that you - as a person - understand the situation, even if your character may not. That keeps any IC blame firmly in the IC where it belongs.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nIf my character doesn\'t care about the innocent NPCs he just killed by lighting the stables on fire in a session, how can other players tell that\'s not my own opinion too?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \"They can\'t! I need to include an OOC note to make sure they know for sure that I know what my character did was wrong.\"\n    a2: \"They should be able to tell because I\'m not a bad person.\"\n    a3: \"It doesn\'t matter if they know or not, it\'s not like their opinion of me matters.\"\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_10:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Respecting boundaries\'\n  \'#description\': \"People don’t like being randomly touched by strangers in real life. That’s a concept we’re taught from an early age. The same applies in roleplaying as well.<br>\\n<br>\\nA scene you are in with another player may make them uncomfortable. That’s not your fault, but they are not obligated to stick around for it. Especially not in an online environment where they may simply logoff. The player does  not have to validate the existence of their emotions regardless of how much others didn’t intend the results, or the logic or lack thereof in having them. (That’s why they’re called emotions.)<br>\\n<br>\\nIf someone asks for a scene to stop, or has their character firmly walk away without giving any indication they are wanting you to stop them, take the hint and let them walk away.<br>\\n<br>\\nIf you are confused about what happened, ask later after they have had time to calm down. It is best to do that in private over PMs. The problem may not have had anything to do with you, or it may be something they aren’t comfortable talking about in chat so putting them on the spot in public isn’t going to help.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nWhat sort of topics are likely to make other people uncomfortable? (Check all that apply)\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': checkboxes\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \'Child abuse\'\n    a2: Sex\n    a3: \'Rape (of any variety)\'\n    a4: \'Bigotry (racism or sexism)\'\n    a5: Fights\n    a6: Disablity\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_11:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Respecting work\'\n  \'#description\': \"A/SHs go to a lot of effort to run games here and they are all volunteer. There’s a lot that goes into running in a shared game setting without stepping on each others toes, and a lot of work to keep from treating other player’s characters unfairly. They have to be trained, take tests, and work through multiple sessions before they are allowed to run freely. Many of our A/SHs take a good chunk of their time before and after a session to plan it, write up the report for after, and plan for any future sessions that may occur as fall out.<br>\\n<br>\\nThat sort of training doesn’t happen overnight, so please respect the work that our A/SH undertake to bring stories to life for the site.<br>\\n<br>\\nIf a specific session doesn’t ‘fit’ for your character concept then see if you can find a way for the character to fit, bring another character, or bow out gracefully. Don’t try to make the A/SH feel like they’re responsible for actions that are ultimately under your control - it’s your character and your fingers typing at the keyboard.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nIf I can\'t go to a session because my character would be asleep at that time, what should I do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \'Decide my character has insomnia that night, so I can be taking a walk near the location for the session.\'\n    a2: \'Ask A/SH privately for ideas on how to get my character there.\'\n    a3: \"Complain in Limbo about how you can\'t make it because of the timing.\"\n    a4: \"Nothing - don\'t go. If they want you there, they\'ll make better arrangements next time.\"\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_12:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Respecting time\'\n  \'#description\': \"Our A/SHs aren’t paid. But they’re also not independently wealthy either - that means for the most part they have jobs. So be patient with requests, especially if there’s a lot of other activity on the site at the time. We try to handle everything within 72 hours (that’s 3 days). Usually we get to it sooner - but sometimes there’s just too much to get done.<br>\\n<br>\\nBeing mindful of the time also applies to sessions - while we know sessions will run over. And they do more often than not - if you then get into a PvP fight immediately after - understand you’re tying up an A/SH who may already be past their bedtime for potentially another four to five hours of play. They need to sleep sometime!<br>\\n<br>\\nIn those situations, offer to pause the situation and schedule it for a better time - your A/SH and your fellow players will be grateful for the courtesy.<br>\\n<br>\\nWe span across multiple timezones - it can be tricky to make arrangements, and of course adding in work, school, and personal schedules - sometimes it’s just not possible for an A/SH to match your schedule to theirs. It’s not personal.<br>\\n<br>\\nIf there is an A/SH or a player you want to play with and you can’t find a time that works, consider inviting them to a slow post.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nIf a session starts an hour before you get off work, what should you do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \"Make sure my availability is posted on the forums for planning, I\'ll catch the next one.\"\n    a2: \"Complain privately to the A/SH that they didn\'t shift the timeframe for me.\"\n    a3: \"Complain in Limbo to the A/SH that they didn\'t shift the timeframe for me.\"\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_13:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Pushing boundaries\'\n  \'#description\': \"Roleplaying can push many boundaries on occasion, but the question always is how far is too far? There is no easy answer to this as different people have placed the line in different places. This site, however, is about promoting fun as well as being a safe place to come and play. We have already covered respecting each other’s boundaries as different people have their too far line in different places - but there are some things that should be avoid all together in character - and more importantly - OUT of character.<br>\\n<br>\\nStart with the <a href=\\\"/wiki/code-conduct\\\">Code of Conduct</a> and the site <a href=\\\"/wiki/harassment-policy\\\">Harassment Policy<a>, those pages will let you know  if certain scenes and topics are not acceptable for roleplay on the site. The second thing you can do is ask yourself - how far is too far? If you are unsure, before you post anything seek out an A/SH and talk to them. After talking to an A/SH make sure to clear it with your fellow players as well.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nWhat behavior should I avoid in IC and OCC for out-of-character reasons per Site Policy? (check all that apply)</a></a>\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': checkboxes\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: Stalking\n    a2: \'Underage sex\'\n    a3: \'Nonconsensual sexual behavior\'\n    a4: Hate-speech\n    a5: \'Rough language\'\n    a6: \'Politics and religion\'\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_14:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Sharing\'\n  \'#description\': \"We are all responsible for making sure other people have fun and get to share the limelight as well. Share information unless there’s a reason not to - in general the more you help support other players in the session, the more they can support you. Look for opportunities to shine the spotlight on others.<br>\\n<br>\\nIf your character has many skills he should have many opportunities to shine in a session. But you know that one of the other characters has a similar skillset - and only that skillset - when it comes up, make an effort to defer to the character that genuinely has no other role in the session. Otherwise you’re leaving a player sitting bored while you claim the one opportunity they had to shine.<br>\\n<br>\\nThat’s just being polite and letting them have a slice of fun too.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nIf you\'re playing an all-around wizard expert in healing, fire-balls, and animal control, and another player is playing a beast-master concept, what situation in a session should you let the beast-master take lead on so they can have their moment in the session?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \'Calming the animal guards\'\n    a2: \'Dealing direct damage to the bad guy with spells\'\n    a3: \'Healing the party during or after the fight\'\n    a4: \"Any of them, it\'s the beast-master\'s responsibility to expand his skills.\"\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_15:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Handling disappointment\'\n  \'#description\': \"Disappointment is bound to happen just as it happens in real life. Sometimes the dice are not nice or someone\'s character did something that mess up your character\'s plan. It is just like our own troubles we encounter in life. It is ok to feel disappointment, but there are ways to handle it correctly. <br>\\n<br>\\nDon\'t just throw tantrums. If you need to, take a breather. Then if you feel the need to express in public don’t blame, try to express your issue and offer reasonable solutions to fix it. If you are unable to, then seek out one of our A/SHs - they may be able to work with you to get to that point. <br>\\n<br>\\nTry and see the whole picture, often its just a matter of perspective. Try and step back and look at everything: Did your expectations vastly differ from everyone else\'s? What was the belief behind your expectations? Is your expectation a reasonable or a likely outcome? If you are having trouble trying to see all sides of the situation then seek out one of our A/SHs - they may be able to help you see the other angles.<br>\\n<br>\\nLearning from disappointment. This can often be the hardest thing to do. But once you have a cooler head try and look back at the situation, is there something you can learn from it? Often it could have just been a misunderstanding so next time you know to ask for a little more clarification before you think on how your character acts.<br>\\n<br>\\nLastly there is acceptance. Not all situations that lead to disappointment can be changed. It is important to work through your disappointment, see all sides, see what there is to learn, accept what happened and then begin the process of moving on.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nIf another character decides not to go along with your plan, so your character ended up dead, injured or in jail, what should you do?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \"I\'m the one who decided to go through with the plan and didn\'t make sure the other player was up for it. I get what I get.\"\n    a2: \"I should complain to the player off-site for not backing me up - it\'s his fault my character got hurt.\"\n    a3: \"I should complain to the player in Limbo for not backing me up - it\'s his fault my character got hurt.\"\n    a4: \"I shouldn\'t do anything, the character is just screwed by the other player and the A/SH.\"\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_16:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - How to complain\'\n  \'#description\': \"Something happened in a session or a scene and it\'s just not sitting right with you. You may be considering calling Real Life Harm, or you may not be getting along with another player, or even with a particular A/SH. It happens, even if we try to avoid it. Don\'t just let it simmer, that just makes things worse!<br>\\n<br>\\nHere\'s what you do:<br>\\n<br>\\nFirst, stop and think! Wait until you\'re calmer and reconsider the situation. Are you still sure you were wronged or was it more a misunderstanding?<br>\\n<br>\\nSecond, talk to the player involved (if you can). Try to get it sorted out amongst yourselves. Be prepared to hear some hard things, or be asked to take specific action.<br>\\n<br>\\nThird, if you can\'t work it out with the player - then talk to an A/SH. The A/SHs are there for that. They may pass you up to one of the other A/SH to handle the situation.<br>\\n<br>\\nFourth, an A/SH may ask you to take specific actions in response to the problem. It\'s possible you might actually be at fault yourself, if so take the time to really listen to what the A/SH is telling you needs to be done to fix the situation.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nIf another player is having problems with me, what\'s the second thing you should try if you don\'t know what started it?\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \"Talk to the other player to find out what\'s going on.\"\n    a2: \"Ignore it, he\'ll get over it.\"\n    a3: \'Ask an A/SH to intervene.\'\n    a4: \"Fight back - he started it, I\'ll end it.\"\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_17:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Anon mailer\'\n  \'#description\': \"Sometimes the problem might be an A/SH, or a Head, or even just a particularly sensitive player. You may not feel comfortable saying anything out loud. In that case, that\'s what the anonymous mailer is for. You\'ll find it on the side menu.<br>\\n<br>\\nThe anonymous mailer will send an email to the Heads after scrubbing your email address off the information. That way you can send feedback and not be tracked back. The Heads are required to reply to any mail they receive on the mailer - and because it\'s anonymous, it\'ll be public on the forums.<br>\\n<br>\\nAfter two weeks, the mail will show on the mailer\'s \\\"Posts made\\\" tab. That way the site can always know if any posts to the anonymous mailer have been ignored by the heads and call attention to it if it was something actually important.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nWhat sort of topic is the anon mailer used for? (check all that apply)\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': checkboxes\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \"When I have a problem with an A/SH or Head\'s decision\"\n    a2: \'When I have a problem with another player\'\n    a3: \'When I want a new site feature\'\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_18:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Consequences\'\n  \'#description\': \"We’re pretty casual around here, but when a player goes far enough out of line we do have a way of keeping track of problems. We have a strike system on the site - and usually we’ll try to work with you as much as we can before calling for a strike - but it can happen. They come in two flavors “yellow” and “red”.<br>\\n<br>\\nA yellow strike is a warning. We keep a record of everything that led up to the strike, so all of our A/SHs will be familiar with what happened. You can get up to three Yellow strikes before we ask you to leave the site.<br>\\n<br>\\nA red strike is an instant out. We also keep a record of these and we ban the account.<br>\\n<br>\\nWe usually try to take other approaches before we reach the level of a strike though. You may be asked to take the social training walkthrough, or asked to avoid another character or player in sessions - you may have to alternate who gets to go to sessions to avoid each other if you can’t otherwise get along. If things get particularly bad we may ask you to take a small break from the site to get your thoughts together. A strike is usually our last resort.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Questions:</strong><br>\\nWhat sort of things might an A/SH ask me to do instead of giving me a strike? (check all that apply)\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': checkboxes\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \'Publicly apologize for my actions\'\n    a2: \'Privately apologize for my actions\'\n    a3: \"Avoid being in sessions with another player, we\'ll alternate sessions\"\n    a4: \'Step away from posting in Limbo or in character for a short period of time\'\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_19:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - How to apologize\'\n  \'#description\': \"Apologizing is hard! It really is! But a good apology really can heal many things. The thing is, if you need to apologize it\'s important to actually mean it. And, truthfully, not many of us have been taught how to make a good one and mean it. Here\'s how to properly apologize:<br>\\n<br>\\nThere are four parts:<br>\\n<br>\\n\\\"I’m sorry for…\\\" - be specific, so the person you\'re apologizing to knows that you know what it is that you\'re apologizing for. If you can\'t be specific about the offense, then the \'apology\' is vague - it doesn\'t mean much at all.<br>\\n<br>\\n\\\"This is wrong because…\\\" - this one may take some thought but that\'s the point. You need to be able to say how what you did hurt another person. \'I\'m sorry you\'re upset\' doesn\'t cut it. This is when you show the other person that you understand what was wrong about your actions.<br>\\n<br>\\n\\\"In the future, I will…\\\" - apologies mean nothing if you turn around the next day and do it again! That\'s not being sorry, that\'s just trying to smooth things over long enough to do it again. State what you will do in the future, not what you won\'t - that way you have a goal in mind.<br>\\n<br>\\n\\\"Will you forgive me?\\\" - this is the offer to fix your friendship. The other person is not obliged to accept! (Nor are you should you be the one being apologized to.) Sometimes they just aren\'t interested in forgiveness, but if you\'ve gone through all four steps then you\'ve made a good effort at it.<br>\\n<br>\\nEach of these serves a very important purpose, so you\'ve got to hit all of them on the way. And yes, it\'s an awkward, uncomfortable, and potentially humiliating thing to do. But if you genuinely owe someone an apology - then you\'ve hurt them. Don\'t you think a little bit of discomfort in return is called for?<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nWhich of these apologies count as a \'real\' one? (check all that apply)\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': checkboxes\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \"I\'m sorry I made you uncomfortable when I joked in Limbo about OCD. This is wrong because it\'s a serious topic that affects people in your life and has hurt them and you. In the future I will make sure that I am considerate of those in the room that I don\'t know may have been affected by it. Will you forgive me?\"\n    a2: \"I\'m sorry for making you angry. This is wrong because I hurt your feelings. In the future I try to be nicer. Will you forgive me?\"\n    a3: \"I\'m sorry for making you feel attacked when I said you shouldn\'t talk about my character. This is wrong because that\'s not how I meant it. In the future I will try to be more clear. Will you forgive me?\"\n    a4: \"I\'m sorry for pestering you about RPing with me all the time. This is wrong because you have a life, and I should respect that. In the future I will not bother you anymore. Will you forgive me?\"\n    a5: \"I\'m sorry for making you feel attacked when I said you shouldn\'t talk about my character. This is wrong because you got angry. In the future I will try to be more clear. Will you forgive me?\"\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nquestion_20:\n  \'#title\': \'Social walkthrough - Site Policies\'\n  \'#description\': \"Our site policies can be found on the wiki under <a href=\\\"/wiki/site-policies\\\">Site Policies</a>. New players to the site should take time to look over this section of the site in particular. There’s a lot of really good information in there.<br>\\n<br>\\nThe <a href=\\\"/wiki/code-conduct\\\">Code of Conduct</a>, as you’ve seen on other sites, should give you a good understanding of the behavior expected of our site members. “<a href=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t3ICRoe-QIw\\\">Wheaton\'s Law</a>” also sums it up nicely.<br>\\n<br>\\nWe also have a <a href=\\\"/wiki/harassment-policy\\\">Site Harassment</a> policy. While the Code of Conduct covers most behaviors, the harassment policy goes into more detail for player interactions and covers specific circumstances.<br>\\n<br>\\nWe also have a rule on the site called <a href=\\\"/wiki/real-life-harm\\\">Real Life Harm</a>. You might see it abbreviated as “RLH”. When something in a scene or session goes way over your personal boundaries and risks driving you off the site, or to abandon the character, you can invoke real life harm to ask the A/SHs to adjust the situation back to something you’re more comfortable with. Real life harm is not about avoiding disappointing outcomes in a session, and we will notice if you abuse it for that.<br>\\n<br>\\n<strong>Question:</strong><br>\\nI have read the Code of Conduct and Site Harassment policies.\"\n  \'#description_display\': before\n  \'#type\': radios\n  \'#options\':\n    a1: \'Yes\'\n    a2: \'No\'\n  \'#options_randomize\': true\n  \'#required\': true\nactions:\n  \'#submit__label\': Finish\n  \'#type\': webform_actions\n  \'#title\': \'Submit button(s)\'\n\";s:3:\"css\";s:0:\"\";s:10:\"javascript\";s:0:\"\";s:8:\"settings\";a:134:{s:4:\"ajax\";b:0;s:15:\"ajax_scroll_top\";s:4:\"form\";s:18:\"ajax_progress_type\";s:0:\"\";s:11:\"ajax_effect\";s:0:\"\";s:10:\"ajax_speed\";N;s:4:\"page\";b:1;s:16:\"page_submit_path\";s:0:\"\";s:17:\"page_confirm_path\";s:0:\"\";s:15:\"page_theme_name\";s:0:\"\";s:10:\"form_title\";s:4:\"both\";s:16:\"form_submit_once\";b:0;s:17:\"form_open_message\";s:0:\"\";s:18:\"form_close_message\";s:0:\"\";s:22:\"form_exception_message\";s:0:\"\";s:25:\"form_previous_submissions\";b:0;s:17:\"form_confidential\";b:0;s:25:\"form_confidential_message\";s:0:\"\";s:24:\"form_disable_remote_addr\";b:0;s:22:\"form_convert_anonymous\";b:0;s:16:\"form_prepopulate\";b:0;s:30:\"form_prepopulate_source_entity\";b:0;s:39:\"form_prepopulate_source_entity_required\";b:0;s:35:\"form_prepopulate_source_entity_type\";s:0:\"\";s:12:\"form_unsaved\";b:0;s:17:\"form_disable_back\";b:0;s:16:\"form_submit_back\";b:0;s:25:\"form_disable_autocomplete\";b:0;s:15:\"form_novalidate\";b:0;s:26:\"form_disable_inline_errors\";b:0;s:13:\"form_required\";b:0;s:14:\"form_autofocus\";b:0;s:19:\"form_details_toggle\";b:0;s:10:\"form_reset\";b:0;s:18:\"form_access_denied\";s:7:\"default\";s:24:\"form_access_denied_title\";s:0:\"\";s:26:\"form_access_denied_message\";s:0:\"\";s:29:\"form_access_denied_attributes\";a:0:{}s:15:\"form_file_limit\";s:0:\"\";s:23:\"form_file_limit_message\";s:0:\"\";s:15:\"form_attributes\";a:0:{}s:11:\"form_method\";s:0:\"\";s:11:\"form_action\";s:0:\"\";s:5:\"share\";b:0;s:10:\"share_node\";b:0;s:16:\"share_theme_name\";s:0:\"\";s:11:\"share_title\";b:1;s:26:\"share_page_body_attributes\";a:0:{}s:16:\"submission_label\";s:0:\"\";s:28:\"submission_exception_message\";s:0:\"\";s:25:\"submission_locked_message\";s:0:\"\";s:14:\"